Anijsmelk:
{{Short description|Dutch milk drink}}
'''''Anijsmelk''''' is a Dutch drink consisting of hot milk flavored with [[anise]] seed and sweetened with sugar. One traditional use takes place during ice skating.<ref name="Bender2009">{{cite book|last=Bender|first=David A.|title=A Dictionary of Food and Nutrition|url=https://archive.org/details/dictionaryoffood00bend|url-access=registration|accessdate=5 August 2013|year=2009|publisher=Oxford UP|isbn=9780191579752|page=[https://archive.org/details/dictionaryoffood00bend/page/272 272]}}</ref> ''Anijsmelk'' is also said to have a [[soporific]] effect. The drink (which, outside the Netherlands, is consumed by [[expatriate|expat]]s in, for instance, [[Dutch Americans|Michigan]] and [[White South African|South Africa]]) can be made from scratch, but it is more common to use tablets called ''anijsblokjes'', made with anise flavoring and sugar; the tablets are dissolved in hot milk.<ref name="Zanger2001">{{cite book|last=Zanger|first=Mark|title=The American Ethnic Cookbook for Students|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=JTBSpuCkl9AC&pg=PA311|accessdate=5 August 2013|year=2001|publisher=ABC-CLIO|isbn=9781573563451|page=311}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.food.com/recipe/anise-milk-anijsmelk-159203|title=Anise Milk - Anijsmelk Recipe|date=9 March 2006|publisher=Food.com|accessdate=5 August 2013}}</ref><ref name="Vries2011">{{cite book|last=Vries|first=Dolf de|title=Zuid-Afrika|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=xHg55hDTNUIC&pg=PT54|accessdate=5 August 2013|year=2011|publisher=Lannoo Meulenhoff|isbn=9789047520313|page=54}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thedutchtable.com/2012/01/anijsmelk.html|title=Anijsmelk|date=22 January 2012|publisher=The Dutch Table|language=Dutch|accessdate=5 August 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://travel-wonders.com/2011/04/02/drinks-around-the-world-anijsmelk-netherlands/|title=Drinks Around the World: Anijsmelk (Netherlands)|date=2 April 2011|publisher=Travel Wonders of the World|accessdate=5 August 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130501031302/http://travel-wonders.com/2011/04/02/drinks-around-the-world-anijsmelk-netherlands/|archive-date=1 May 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref> ''Anijsblokjes'' are made by the company De Ruijter and have been produced since at least the 19th century.<ref>{{cite news|title=Anijsmelk-Tabletten |url=http://www.groenehartarchieven.nl/kranten/rijnbode/1894-12-12 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130805193747/http://www.groenehartarchieven.nl/kranten/rijnbode/1894-12-12 |url-status=dead |archive-date=5 August 2013 |date=12 December 1894 |publisher=[[De Rijnbode]] |page=2 |language=Dutch |accessdate=5 August 2013 }}</ref> However, the machine that was used to make these aniseed and sugar blocks broke down and was irreparable due to age. Since then, the product has been sold in powder sachets.<ref name="Vragen">{{cite web|url=http://www.deruijter.nl/vragen|title=Vragen|last=|first=|date=|website=|publisher=De Ruyter|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221114005745/https://www.deruijter.nl/vragen|archive-date=14 November 2022|language=Dutch|accessdate=20 April 2020}}</ref>

