Herbsaint:
{{Short description|Brand name of anise-flavored liquor}}
'''Herbsaint''' is a [[brand name]] of [[anise]]-flavored [[liqueur]] originally created as an [[absinthe]] substitute in [[New Orleans, Louisiana]] in 1934,<ref name=StoryOfHerbsaint>Jay Hendrickson, [http://www.oxygenee.com/absinthe-america/herbsaint.html Absinthe in America I - The Story of Herbsaint] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101225070014/http://www.oxygenee.com/absinthe-america/herbsaint.html |date=2010-12-25 }}, ''The Virtual Absinthe Museum'' at Oxygénée Ltd. (Access date December 7, 2010.)</ref> and currently produced by the [[Sazerac Company]].

It was developed by J. Marion Legendre and Reginald Parker of the city, who had learned how to make absinthe while in [[France]] during [[World War I]].<ref name = StoryOfHerbsaint/> It was originally produced under the name "Legendre [[Absinthe]]", although it never contained absinthe's essential ingredient, [[Artemisia absinthium|Grande Wormwood]] ''(Artemisia absinthium)''.  It first went on sale following the repeal of [[Prohibition]], and was unique in its category as an absinthe substitute, as opposed to a [[pastis]].<ref name = StoryOfHerbsaint/>    The Federal Alcohol Control Administration soon objected to Legendre's use of the word "absinthe",<ref>[http://www.oxygenee.com/images/New-Orleans-Absinthe.jpg Absinthe Banned: Ruling Given by Analyst] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110104095529/http://oxygenee.com/images/New-Orleans-Absinthe.jpg |date=2011-01-04 }}, reprint from ''New Orleans Item'', May 6, 1934. (Access date December 7, 2010.)</ref> so the name was changed to "Legendre ''Herbsaint''",  French/Creole for "Herbe Sainte" (Sacred Herb), the ''Artemisia absinthium''. 

[[Sazerac Company|The Sazerac Company]] bought J.M. Legendre & Co. in June 1949.<ref>{{Cite web |title=This Prohibition-Era Spirit Still Reigns Supreme In New Orleans |url=https://www.southernliving.com/new-orleans-prohibition-liquor-herbsaint-11859723 |access-date=2026-02-13 |website=Southern Living |language=en}}</ref> Herbsaint was originally bottled at 120 proof (60%), but this was later reduced to 100 proof (50%), then changed to a different 90 proof (45%) recipe in the mid-1950s. By the early 1970s only the 90 proof remained.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Clarke |first=Paul |date=2009-01-20 |title=Elements: Herbsaint |url=https://imbibemagazine.com/elements-herbsaint/ |access-date=2026-02-13 |website=Imbibe Magazine |language=en-US}}</ref> In December 2009, the Sazerac Company reintroduced J.M. Legendre's original 100 proof recipe as ''Herbsaint Original''.<ref>Todd A. Price, [http://www.nola.com/drink/index.ssf/2009/12/sazerac_co_reintroduces_the_or.html Sazerac Co. reintroduces the original recipe for Herbsaint], ''The Times-Picayune'', December 19, 2009. (Access date December 6, 2010.)</ref>

