Koko Samoa:
{{Short description|Samoan hot chocolate drink}}
{{About|the drink|the wrestler|Cocoa Samoa}}
'''Koko Samoa''' is a traditional Samoan [[hot chocolate]] drink made from ground [[cocoa bean]]s. Most [[Cocoa production in Samoa|cocoa produced in Samoa]] is consumed domestically as koko Samoa, which is sold at local markets by cocoa farmers and their families. Koko Samoa may also refer to a dry [[Cocoa solids|cocoa mass]] made from coarsely ground cocoa beans, or a mixed subspecies of cocoa known more commonly as Samoan [[Types of cocoa beans#Trinitario|Trinitario]].

== Background ==
The cocoa tree (''[[Theobroma cacao]]'') is not native to [[Samoa]] but was introduced to the islands in 1883 by [[German colonial empire|German colonists]] who brought plants of the [[Types of cocoa beans#Criollo|Criollo]] subspecies from [[British Ceylon]] (present-day [[Sri Lanka]]).<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Dillon |first1=Natalie L. |last2=Zhang |first2=Dapeng |last3=Nauheimer |first3=Lars |last4=Toramo |first4=Elison |last5=Nagalevu |first5=Paitia |last6=Melteras |first6=Marie-Vianney |last7=Wallez |first7=Sandrine |last8=Finau |first8=Kuinimeri |last9=Nakidakida |first9=Sefanaia |last10=Lepou |first10=Petelo |last11=Diczbalis |first11=Yan |title=Understanding the cocoa genetic resources in the Pacific to assist producers to supply the growing craft market |journal=New Zealand Journal of Crop and Horticultural Science |date=1 October 2024 |volume=52 |issue=4 |pages=306–320 |doi=10.1080/01140671.2023.2278788 |bibcode=2024NZJCH..52..306D |url=https://doi.org/10.1080/01140671.2023.2278788 |access-date=22 August 2025 |issn=0114-0671|url-access=subscription }}</ref>{{rp|307}} Although the Germans intended for the cocoa to be grown mainly as an [[Cash crop|export crop]], Samoans adopted it into their culture soon thereafter, and cocoa presently sees large domestic consumption in Samoa, unlike elsewhere in [[Oceania]].<ref name="OCOP factsheets">{{cite web |title=OCOP factsheets: Samoa / Cocoa |url=https://openknowledge.fao.org/server/api/core/bitstreams/9a77e780-a875-4470-a564-88738e16de16/content |date=2025 |website=One Country One Priority Product (OCOP) |publisher=[[Food and Agriculture Organization]] |access-date=25 August 2025 |language=en}}</ref>{{rp|1}}

== Preparation ==
Most cocoa produced in Samoa is consumed locally as koko Samoa.<ref name="PCEIO">{{cite web |title=Pacific Cocoa Export Industry Overview |url=https://phamaplus.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Cocoa_FS_Overview6-1.pdf |website=Pacific Horticultural and Agricultural Market Access Program |publisher=[[Government of Australia]] |access-date=22 August 2025 |date=July 2024}}</ref>{{rp|3}} It is typically prepared by women.<ref name="OCOP factsheets"/>{{rp|2}} Koko Samoa is made first by harvesting ripe cocoa pods from a cocoa tree, and separating the seeds (i.e. cocoa beans) from the surrounding pulp. The beans are then fermented, sun-dried, and roasted over a hot metal plate heated by an open fire. After roasting, the husks are removed from the beans, which are subsequently ground into a coarse liquid paste in a wooden [[Mortar and pestle|mortar]]. The paste is then mixed with boiling water in a kettle and sweetened to make the drink.<ref name="OCOP factsheets"/>{{rp|4}}<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Roufs |first=Timothy G |title=Sweet Treats Around the World: An Encyclopedia of Food and Culture |last2=Smyth Roufs |first2=Kathleen |publisher=[[ABC-CLIO]] |year=2014 |isbn=978-1-61069-220-5 |location=Santa Barbara, California}}</ref>{{Rp|page=261}}

== Consumption and other uses ==
Cocoa farmers and their families typically make and sell the koko Samoa drink at local markets.<ref name="PCEIO"/> Grounded cocoa beans are often turned into a shelf-stable [[Cocoa solids|cocoa mass]] also called koko Samoa, which can be stored and turned into the drink at a later time. The mass is in year-round demand locally, and is also exported in significant amounts to [[New Zealand]], [[Australia]], and [[American Samoa]].<ref name="OCOP factsheets"/>{{rp|2–3}} In preparing the drink, cooks scrape or grate cocoa off the block, allowing it to boil for 15 minutes. The drink is then combined with sugar and sometimes milk, quickly consumed before it can cool, as fat separates and rises to the top. The chocolate grounds left in koko Samoa are either eaten or strained out through the drinker's teeth.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=261}}

Koko Samoa is also an alternative name for Samoan [[Types of cocoa beans#Trinitario|Trinitario]], a hybrid of [[Types of cocoa beans#Criollo|Criollo]] and [[Types of cocoa beans#Forastero|Forastero]] cocoa plants brought from Sri Lanka and [[Java]], respectively.<ref name="Faasau 2023">{{cite news |last=Faasau |first=Gutu |title=Push to increase Samoan cocoa exports |url=https://www.samoaobserver.ws/category/samoa/106493 |access-date=22 August 2025 |work=[[Samoa Observer]] |date=1 November 2023 |language=en}}</ref>

