A '''Roy Rogers''' is a [[List of non-alcoholic mixed drinks|non-alcoholic mixed drink]] made with [[cola]] and [[grenadine]] syrup,<ref>{{cite web|first=Colleen | last=Graham |title=Roy Rogers Mocktail Recipe - Mixed Drink Recipe for a Non-Alcoholic Roy Rogers|url=http://cocktails.about.com/od/cocktailrecipes/r/roy_rgr_mktl.htm|publisher= | website= cocktails.about.com|accessdate=14 October 2014|date= 2010-04-08| archive-date=2014-10-19|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141019011212/http://cocktails.about.com/od/cocktailrecipes/r/roy_rgr_mktl.htm|url-status=dead}}</ref> and traditionally [[Cocktail garnish|garnished]] with a [[maraschino cherry]].

The drink originated in the 1940s and is named after American actor and singer [[Roy Rogers]] (1911–1998), who was popular at the time. It was likely named after Rogers because he did not drink alcohol.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Overhiser |first=Sonja |date= 2021-08-27 |title=Roy Rogers Drink |url=https://www.acouplecooks.com/roy-rogers-drink/ |access-date=2023-02-01 |website=ACoupleCooks.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

The Roy Rogers is similar to the [[Shirley Temple (drink)|Shirley Temple]], which uses [[ginger ale]] and/or [[List of lemon-lime drink brands|lemon-lime soda]] instead of cola. Colas traditionally have more carbonation than ginger ale or lemon-lime sodas. The Roy Rogers was reportedly sold as a boy's alternative to the Shirley Temple in the 1950s and 1960s.<ref name=":0" /> Despite cola being traditionally more popular than ginger ale or lemon-lime sodas, Shirley Temples remain more popular than Roy Rogers, likely due to marketing popularizing the Temple drink.{{citation needed|date=February 2026}}

