Tortuga (cocktail):
{{Short description|Non-alcoholic cocktail beverage}}
A '''Tortuga cocktail''' is a [[non-alcoholic beverage]] named for the [[Haiti]]an island [[Tortuga (Haiti)|Tortuga]] of northern [[Hispaniola]] that was used by pirates as a launching ground for piracy activities.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://havana-club.com/en/bar-news/tortuga-cocktail-0 |title=The Tortuga Cocktail: A Cuban-inspired drink named after a pirate island and served as a Polynesian cocktail in tropical bars the world over |website= havana-club.com |editor=Monti, François |date=6 May 2013 |publisher=Bacardi |accessdate=22 August 2015}}</ref> It is made with iced tea and brown sugar, garnished with cinnamon and a lime wedge.<ref>﻿{{cite web |url= http://www.cocktaildb.com/recipe_detail?id=2329 |title= Tortuga |website= CocktailDB.com| url-status= dead| archiveurl= https://web.archive.org/web/20150715095829/http://www.cocktaildb.com/recipe_detail?id=2329| archivedate= 2015-07-15 |access-date= 2026-04-01}}</ref> It is similar to the popular American drink [[sweet tea]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.4qua.de/alkoholfreie-cocktails-mit-wenig-zutaten/|title=Simple non-alcoholic cocktails with little ingredients| website= 4qua.de| date=2018-06-17|access-date=2018-06-29}}</ref>

An unrelated drink of the same name was served by [[Trader Vic's]] in the 1930s. It was a variation on the [[daiquiri]], made using overproof Guyanese and Cuban rums and small amounts of curacao, creme de cacao, and sweet vermouth for additional flavor.{{Cn|date=November 2023}}

