Toronto (cocktail):
{{short description|Mildly bitter cocktail}}
{{Infobox drink
| name        = Toronto
| image       = The Toronto Cocktail (13570423424).jpg  
| caption     = Toronto cocktail served on the rocks 
| type        = [[List of cocktails|Cocktail]]
| base        = [[Rye whiskey]]
| ingredients ={{plainlist|*2 oz [[rye whisky]]<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cocktaildb.com/recipe_detail?id=2328|archive-date=September 9, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150909230732/http://www.cocktaildb.com/recipe_detail?id=2328|title=Toronto|website=Internet Cocktail Database}}</ref>
*{{frac|1|4}} oz [[Fernet-Branca]]
*{{frac|1|4}} tsp [[sugar]]
*1 dash [[angostura bitters]]}}
| served      = [[Straight up]]: chilled, without ice
| garnish     = [[orange (fruit)|orange]] slice
| drinkware   = [[Cocktail glass]]
}}
The '''Toronto''' is a [[Dryness (taste)|dry]], rich, and mildly bitter [[cocktail]] consisting of [[rye whisky]], [[Fernet-Branca]], [[angostura bitters]], and either [[sugar]] or [[simple syrup]]. An article in [[Gizmodo]] by Brent Rose describes it as the "most popular legit cocktail that uses [[fernet]]".{{sfn|Rose|2013}} It is named after the Canadian city of [[Toronto]].{{sfn|Sismondo|2016}}

==History==
It was first recorded as the ''Fernet Cocktail'' in Robert Vermeire's 1922 edition of ''Cocktails: How to Mix Them'', in which he stated that the "cocktail is much appreciated by the Canadians of Toronto".{{sfn|Soole|Caudle|2013|p=198}} Because the importation of alcoholic beverages to [[Ontario]] was banned, as a result of the [[1921 Ontario prohibition referendum|1921 prohibition referendum]], a reliable source for the Italian-made [[fernet]] would not have been readily available.{{sfn|Sismondo|2016}} It is unclear whether the Torontonians referenced by Vermeire obtained the drink in Toronto before prohibition, or at his bar in [[London]], England.{{sfn|Sismondo|2016}}

By 1930, it was listed as the Toronto Cocktail in ''Cocktail Bill Boothby's World Drinks And How To Mix Them'' by [[William "Cocktail" Boothby]]. The recipes by Vermeire and Boothby both listed equal measures of rye whisky and Fernet-Branca. In 1948, it was listed as the ''Toronto Cocktail'' in the book ''[[The Fine Art of Mixing Drinks]]'' by [[David A. Embury]], and in 1949 a recipe for the Toronto Cocktail was included in ''[[Esquire (magazine)|Esquire]]'s Handbook for Hosts''.{{sfn|Palassio|Wilcox|2005|p=74}}

==Description==
The Toronto Cocktail is a variation of the [[Old fashioned (cocktail)|Old Fashioned]], with the addition of Fernet-Branca.{{sfn|Palassio|Wilcox|2005|p=72}}

Variations of the cocktail substitute various [[rye whiskey]]s as the primary ingredient, such as Canadian whisky{{sfn|Soole|Caudle|2013|p=198}} or [[Old Overholt]].{{sfn|Darlington|Darlington|2016|loc=Toronto Cocktail}} The Fernet Cocktail recipe by Vermeire specified {{convert|1/4|impgi|ml|lk=in}} of [[cognac]] or rye whisky, and {{frac|1|4}} gill of Fernet-Branca.{{sfn|Difford's Guide}}

The rye whisky complements the bitter Fernet-Branca and prevents it from dominating the cocktail.{{sfn|Clarke}} Sugar or simple syrup are used to reduce the cocktail's [[Taste#Bitterness|bitterness]].{{sfn|Darlington|Darlington|2016|loc=Toronto Cocktail}}

It may be served in a [[cocktail glass]] or [[coupe glass]], and served with an orange slice or twist.{{sfn|Darlington|Darlington|2016|loc=Toronto Cocktail}}{{sfn|CocktailDB}} It may also be served with ice in an [[old fashioned glass]].{{sfn|Difford's Guide}} It has a rich flavour with a mild bitterness.{{sfn|Clarke}}

